WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.440
For this video, I wanted to put a bit of a&nbsp;
spotlight onto Bella Ramsey since she’s doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:09.840
a great job in The Last of Us and I wanted to go&nbsp;
back and analyze what she did so well as Lyanna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.940
Mormont. Initially, she was supposed to be a&nbsp;
one-off character, and somehow she was able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:20.880
to convince David and Dan to give her more scenes&nbsp;
and even a major one at the end of season 6. Along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:26.640
with becoming a recurring character afterwards.&nbsp;
This is a rare feat in shows and essentially means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:31.560
that the actor was so good to the point that&nbsp;
they changed the minds of the writers. Another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:37.380
example of this was Jesse Pinkman in Breaking Bad.&nbsp;
Jessie was supposed to die in season 1, but Vince&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:41.700
Gilligan was convinced through Aaron’s performance&nbsp;
that there was something missing within the show.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:47.400
From there he became an integral character and&nbsp;
even ended up getting his own spinoff movie. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:52.920
even though Lyanna Mormont isn’t as important as&nbsp;
Jessie was, Game of Thrones is an inherently big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:57.900
show with dozens of characters so it could have&nbsp;
been really easy for David and Dan to have dropped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:02.880
her and there wouldn't have been a major impact.&nbsp;
So that means that she’d have to be even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:08.640
impressive to push past any potential competition&nbsp;
for screen time. For Lyanna Mormont, Let’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:13.920
identify the core internal and external reasons as&nbsp;
to why she turned out to be such a good character.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:19.500
Starting out with the internal factor, we have&nbsp;
the performance of Bella Ramsey. Game of Thrones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:24.300
has had some amazing child actors and I won’t&nbsp;
go so far to say that Bella Ramsey is the best,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:28.620
but I think she stands out because of how&nbsp;
well she fits into the mold of Lyanna’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:34.080
badass persona. It’s really hard for a child to&nbsp;
command this much respect and to be this bold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:40.200
and it could have easily of not worked. That’s why&nbsp;
to me she’s so distinct and must be why David and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:45.480
Dan wanted to extend her presence within the show.&nbsp;
But let’s look specifically at what Bella Ramsey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:51.420
does to achieve this. Acting wise what sticks out&nbsp;
the most is her usage of inflection and the volume&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.420 --> 00:02:03.780
of her voice. She intentionally puts a lot of&nbsp;
emphasis on certain words to further punctuate a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:09.120
character beat. Along with using this louder shift&nbsp;
in volume to be more commanding and to stand above&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:13.680
the other characters who are more hesitant to&nbsp;
speak louder. It erupts her onto the scene where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:18.120
she breaks the tradition of making a bit of small&nbsp;
talk and cutting to the chase with why they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:23.160
there. I think a clever thing that Bryan Cogman&nbsp;
does with the script was having Lyanna interrupt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:38.700
characters on multiple occasions when she starts&nbsp;
talking. It creates this brief moment that catches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:43.020
the characters off guard and immediately&nbsp;
establishes that we are playing by Lyanna’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:49.020
rules. She’s essentially in charge of this scene.&nbsp;
Another small moment that geniusly sets up a more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:53.760
independent and intelligent characteristic for&nbsp;
Lyanna is the fact that she chooses to confide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:58.380
in her maester for information, but the second&nbsp;
he approaches her without approval she waives him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:03.720
off. Just a very efficient bit of writing and to&nbsp;
be honest this is one of those rare well-written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:09.900
scenes of the later seasons. Then there are even&nbsp;
some instances of subtle cinematography that aid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:15.180
the notion that Lyanna is in charge. For this&nbsp;
shot, the composition places Jon on the right side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:19.980
of the frame and there’s a lot of space behind&nbsp;
him. Since there is barely any room ahead of him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:25.140
It gives off a claustrophobic presence in front of&nbsp;
him and it feels like he’s talking into a wall or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:30.300
there being opposition ahead of him. This breaks&nbsp;
the normal shot composition of having leading room&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:34.800
ahead of someone talking. And then the shot&nbsp;
after this where it cuts to a close-up of Jon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:40.020
as he’s speaking, we assume that we are going to&nbsp;
stay on this shot for a while, but we don’t when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:45.540
Lyanna interrupts him and we quickly cut away.&nbsp;
So between these two shots, we break tradition in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:50.880
footage to establish Lyanna as being in control.&nbsp;
Now I said I was going to talk specifically about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:55.860
Bella Ramsey’s acting, but I think it’s important&nbsp;
to point out the other aspects of production that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.860 --> 00:04:01.320
aid her in this scene. It all comes together to&nbsp;
make a strong introduction for her character. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:05.940
scene may not require a ton of range emotionally&nbsp;
from Bella Ramsey, but that doesn’t mean she’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:10.560
incapable. As especially shown in The Last of&nbsp;
Us where she goes through an entire gambit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:16.020
emotions that she effortlessly breezes through.&nbsp;
She perfectly does the task she was given in Game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:21.780
of Thrones and if anything does an even better&nbsp;
job than what was expected. She doesn’t come off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:26.580
as trying too hard to be intimidating and her&nbsp;
attitude in this scene is smart and calculated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:39.720
She’s not being overly emotional and it feels&nbsp;
like everything she’s saying has purpose. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:44.460
even though she’s interrupting characters, it&nbsp;
never comes across as that disrespectful because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:48.900
she’s normally the one prioritizing what should be&nbsp;
discussed in the conversation and is smart enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:55.020
to see that this attempt at small talk is futile.&nbsp;
Looking at her final scene of season 6, she gives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.020 --> 00:05:00.780
a rousing speech that leads into the climax of&nbsp;
Jon’s entire season arc. Like that’s honestly an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:05.580
insane upgrade from being a one-off character to&nbsp;
one that has to build up the hype to announce Jon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:11.340
Snow as being King of the North. And I think she&nbsp;
nails this scene really well. Again her usage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:16.560
inflection is very particular and detailed, but I&nbsp;
also love how much she pauses between some of her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:21.420
lines. It creates a sense of dramatic buildup&nbsp;
for her delivery to have even more of a punch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:33.840
when she’s calling out different lords for not&nbsp;
showing up to the Battle of the Bastards. Then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:38.880
the writing aids her acting because she’s able to&nbsp;
escalate a recurring phrase throughout the scene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:44.820
It’s as if she’s embodying a viper as she strikes&nbsp;
down each one of her victims and then she caps off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:50.160
the moment with a triumphant approval of Jon Snow.&nbsp;
In season 8 we have the mirror of this scene which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:55.140
is kind of a nice moment, but it feels a little&nbsp;
cheap that David and Dan are essentially copying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.140 --> 00:06:00.720
this moment from season 6 but it’s in reverse.&nbsp;
Along with it being pretty unreasonable that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:05.400
the North is mad at him for leaving temporarily&nbsp;
to get reinforcements to fight the Night King.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:10.800
Which was completely necessary, but going into all&nbsp;
that would be getting off-topic. Bella Ramsey’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:16.080
acting has well-timed character beats, is able&nbsp;
to captivate an entire room of people with her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:21.540
commanding voice, and she’s able to come across&nbsp;
as simultaneously intelligent and giving off a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:26.340
crazy amount of confidence that really shouldn’t&nbsp;
be possible for a child to portray. And the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:33.060
that a child actor is able to achieve all of this&nbsp;
and so much more is extremely impressive. So I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:37.560
think she perfectly executes the character&nbsp;
Lyanna Mormont from an acting standpoint.

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:41.940
Looking at the more external reasons for&nbsp;
why Lyanna Mormont is a good character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:47.400
there are quite a few. Firstly, I think one of&nbsp;
the most important factors is that she symbolizes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:53.400
what the later seasons lack so much which are more&nbsp;
new characters. David and Dan in general for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:58.620
later seasons wanted to minimize new characters&nbsp;
and focused primarily on the pre-existing ones.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:03.840
Instead of committing to a 10-season goal that&nbsp;
George and HBO wanted, David and Dan opted to rush&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:08.760
through the finish line with 8 seasons that were&nbsp;
also shortened. An example of them not introducing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:14.460
new characters is them reducing plotlines in Dorne&nbsp;
and the Iron Islands. For those two areas, there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:19.560
were much bigger conflicts within the books that&nbsp;
also brought about more characters. Like it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:24.120
have been much cooler if we saw more greyjoys in&nbsp;
the Iron Islands that Euron could kill to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:28.800
him more intimidating. This all highlights a core&nbsp;
issue within the later seasons of game of thrones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:32.400
in which instead of retaining the big scale of&nbsp;
Westeros that the first 4 seasons had, David&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:37.980
and Dan subtly reduced it by not making more&nbsp;
additions. So this makes the inclusion and high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:43.140
quality of Lyanna Mormont that much more important&nbsp;
because she’s a representation of the expansion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:47.460
within the world that should have been more&nbsp;
abundant. From the characters that they did add,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:51.960
Lyanna Mormont is the best in my opinion.&nbsp;
Now you’d think that wouldn’t be the case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:56.880
but try to think about what new characters&nbsp;
David and Dan added in seasons 6,7, and 8&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:04.260
who were better. You basically just have Euron&nbsp;
Greyjoy, Lady Crane, Randyll Tarly, Dickon Tarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:10.440
Archmaester Ebrose, Robett Glover, and the&nbsp;
most important character Captain Strickland.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:17.040
Between these characters, the only one that really&nbsp;
stands out is Randyll Tarly and you could make a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:21.240
case for liking him more than Lyanna because&nbsp;
of his complicated relationship with Samwell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:26.460
but I still like Lyanna more because she has more&nbsp;
to offer in comparison. Lyanna is essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:31.440
a gateway into us seeing more of the world of&nbsp;
Westeros. We’ve never been to bear island before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:36.660
and house mormont is pretty important considering&nbsp;
that Sir Jorah and Geor Mormont play big roles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.660 --> 00:08:42.180
in the overall story. This is the content that&nbsp;
should be elaborated on. Show us more northern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:48.060
houses and flesh everyone out. Especially on each&nbsp;
side of the coin. Because looking at the Battle of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:53.220
the Bastards, Jon Umber and Harald Karstark were&nbsp;
basically background characters who had minuscule&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:58.560
character work. It’s just wild to me that Lyanna&nbsp;
Mormont is one of the only new characters that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:03.900
tangible personality traits and is tied in with&nbsp;
the plot. Like even Lord Glover who I thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:09.360
had some good scenes in season 6 ended up getting&nbsp;
tossed into the bin for season 8. He apparently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:14.220
doesn’t show up and there are no consequences&nbsp;
for that since it’s never revisited. Unless if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:18.480
the Night King attacked him first, but if the&nbsp;
Night King hit every Northern Kingdom north of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:23.700
Winterfell then that would have taken ages. So it&nbsp;
sucks how David and Dan are shrinking the size of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:28.320
the cast and Robett Glover could have easily been&nbsp;
a character to pad out the deaths for The Long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:33.540
Night. When you look at the earlier seasons of the&nbsp;
show, you had a much better roster of characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:39.180
and they also made massive decisions that affected&nbsp;
the story. An example being Rickard Karstark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:44.520
murdering some Lannister boys that then prompted&nbsp;
Rob Stark to execute him which then alienated the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:49.920
Karstark army. And that even leads into Rob Stark&nbsp;
having to stoop so low as to ask help from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:55.140
Freys. Like would you look at that, characters&nbsp;
making big choices that drastically impact what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.140 --> 00:10:00.360
happens. Lyanna doesn’t really have any major&nbsp;
decisions and is mostly along for the ride,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:05.880
but at least her character achieves the bare&nbsp;
minimum for some form of representation of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:10.140
North. She’s entertaining not just because&nbsp;
of Bella Ramsey’s captivating performance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.140 --> 00:10:15.180
but because it gives you a small ounce of&nbsp;
expansion for the north. If we had her and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:20.400
like 3 other prominent northern lords then hey,&nbsp;
maybe we’d be able to have more engaging dialogue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:24.900
scenes within Winterfell. Similar to the scene&nbsp;
where Lyanna is talking to Ser Jorah Mormont.

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:31.020
Another reason why Bella Ramsey worked so well&nbsp;
as Lyanna Mormont was the irony of her stature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:36.480
power, bravery, and pure big balls as a&nbsp;
kid. In comparison to the other grizzled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:41.100
and battle-hardened characters of the North, she&nbsp;
blew them away in terms of her chad-like energy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:46.260
It made her shutting down these seemingly tough&nbsp;
individuals that much more satisfying and boosted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:51.060
her up as being more likable. It basically set her&nbsp;
character up as being inherently entertaining from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:55.860
a writing standpoint and then Bella Ramsey took&nbsp;
that character and brought it to life with great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.860 --> 00:11:01.260
expertise. And I also love how she’s not afraid to&nbsp;
get her hands dirty and wants to become a warrior.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:06.240
It further gives off an angle of feministic&nbsp;
empowerment because if this little girl is ready&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:11.760
to fight and isn’t scared then surely you should&nbsp;
not be. It’s also interesting how David and Dan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:16.140
made a more obvious version of this notion&nbsp;
with this random extra girl wanting to fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:21.300
after this guy was scared to fight. It kind of&nbsp;
bashes this message into your face and I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:25.440
it worked perfectly well through Lyanna Mormont as&nbsp;
a character and they didn’t need to spell it out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:30.960
for the audience. Not to mention this girl doesn’t&nbsp;
show up again. So this feels like a deleted scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:35.160
and the point they’re making here is made very&nbsp;
clear in the next episode through Lyanna Mormont&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:40.080
taking down a giant. Lyanna has one of the most&nbsp;
badass death scenes in the entire show because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:45.540
of this. And this is really the only good moment&nbsp;
in the entirety of The Long Night because it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:49.440
the irony of the smallest character bringing&nbsp;
down the biggest opponent on the battlefield.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:54.720
Although it’s questionable as to why this giant&nbsp;
would pick up Lyanna and stare at her like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:59.700
but whatever, it makes for a good moment if&nbsp;
you don’t question anything. So in essence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:04.140
I think her character is inherently likable&nbsp;
because of how much she embodies a warrior which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.140 --> 00:12:09.600
is also a common trend in game of thrones. Like&nbsp;
through Brienne of Tarth and Arya Stark. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:13.560
it’s just as commendable in Lyanna&nbsp;
because of how much younger she is.

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:19.020
For the last external factor, that benefits Lyanna&nbsp;
Mormont, she offers a good contrast in comparison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:24.060
to the other child characters. For those child&nbsp;
characters we have Joffrey who is a mad and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:30.180
unhinged brat, Sansa who is in constant turmoil&nbsp;
and very slowly over time learns the nature of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.180 --> 00:12:35.100
politicking, Bran was a curious adventurer&nbsp;
turned into a depressed child who finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.100 --> 00:12:39.720
finds purpose in life, Arya who is similar&nbsp;
to Lyanna in that she wants to be a warrior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:44.460
but she has a gaping hole in her heart as&nbsp;
she looks for people to bond to. Lyanna is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.460 --> 00:12:48.360
the most adult-like child out of this bunch&nbsp;
and seeing these qualities within a child&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:53.160
is rare. Making her leadership qualities and&nbsp;
other confident aspects tied to her character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:58.440
that much more fun to watch. She doesn’t feel&nbsp;
like a redundant character because of this and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.440 --> 00:13:03.000
she is able to offer a unique perspective in&nbsp;
the already massive world of game of thrones.

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:08.520
To me, Lyanna Mormont is one of the only new&nbsp;
characters past season 5 that isn’t written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:13.800
poorly. The fact that Bella Ramsey was able&nbsp;
to succeed so well at being a compelling and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:18.840
mature kid who comes across as a leader was&nbsp;
impressive. I’ll also do a video on Oberyn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:23.580
Martell since Pedro Pascal also happens&nbsp;
to be in The Last of Us. In hindsight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:26.940
I probably should have started with him&nbsp;
since he’s a more popular character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:30.540
but I’ll get to it. Thank you for&nbsp;
watching as always and have a good day.

